WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.300
Film Courage: What makes the first draft of a
screenplay crucial to a writer success?

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:10.440
Mark Sanderson, Screenwriter/Author: Hmmmm? I'm glad you asked because there's a
lot of theories going around that the

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:15.509
first draft can be crap you know and it
has to be and I don't believe that it

00:00:15.509 --> 00:00:20.689
has to be crap absolutely not and Ernest
Lehman who'd the great six-time

00:00:20.689 --> 00:00:25.650
nominated Academy Award six-time
nominated screenwriter who did West Side

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:29.970
Story, North by Northwest, Sweet Smell of
Success

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:36.960
he said the first draft is dangerously
important because it's difficult now

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:42.090
this next part is paraphrasing to
totally change something into another

00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:45.840
direction once you've had it down and it
is difficult it's like building a house

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:51.690
and then having the walls don't quite
have the right structure and the windows

00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:54.960
so you know you have to go back in and
now you're dismantling things to try to

00:00:54.960 --> 00:01:00.030
rewrite it and everybody has their own
way of working which is fantastic

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:06.060
whatever's best for you but I always
think why would you waste the time that

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:11.340
it takes to write a first draft and know
that it's not going to be good and know

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:16.530
that 60 70 percent of it is going to be
thrown out or rearranged or maybe not

00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:23.520
even used to get a kernel of of
something that can be used and I always

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:29.130
say by the way when you start working
which is the intent I suppose of

00:01:29.130 --> 00:01:33.810
screenwriters is to work meaning they're
being hired to write scripts that you

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:38.040
know going to me we're produced in two
movies is that you can't turn in a

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:47.100
crappy first draft you'll be fired so on
assignment jobs my first draft has to be

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:51.570
really pretty good to work with it can't
be like well that was a nice pass you

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:57.270
know now we're behind in development you
know sort of and so you want I've

00:01:57.270 --> 00:02:01.259
trained myself over the years to really
it had to have the first draft to be

00:02:01.259 --> 00:02:06.270
really solid because the outline was
really solid and that's another

00:02:06.270 --> 00:02:11.129
discussion about outlines and also
people well I just wing it I just you

00:02:11.129 --> 00:02:15.510
know I sit down I write a script and
then you get lost in page 40 and you

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:20.280
can't really see the rest of where
you're going you know and so it becomes

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:24.300
a muddled mess and it's like what you
know they and I know it sounds that it's

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:29.100
like some kind of formula like well just
do this this and this but sort of when

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:34.050
you start writing professionally there
are steps that's how you're paid to it

00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:39.330
steps so we turn in the first draft and
then we have notes and then we execute

00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:45.060
the second and we move on and on so yes
the first draft I believe is really

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:48.030
important you know and it doesn't have
to be perfect there shouldn't be any

00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:52.410
pressure like I can't write it because I
know it's bad but if you're if you've

00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:56.790
already done like really the heavy
lifting in the outline then the first

00:02:56.790 --> 00:03:01.650
draft Chicago just kind of be a breeze
because you've already kind of in a way

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:07.350
seeing the film worked it out before
you've ever gone two pages you know

00:03:07.350 --> 00:03:12.720
pages of the actual screenplay and I
have found that every time because the

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:16.830
more time you work on the outline the
more details and and again now people

00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:22.110
are gonna say it stifles my creativity
there's no room there's complete room

00:03:22.110 --> 00:03:26.250
for freedom and creativity but you have
a safety net you're not falling off that

00:03:26.250 --> 00:03:31.620
high wire and dropping down the canyon
into the river below your the boom you

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:35.989
you know you've got the net that
captures you and so many things come out

00:03:35.989 --> 00:03:40.170
while I'm writing the script that I
never thought of in the outline because

00:03:40.170 --> 00:03:44.730
I'm in that world and I have that that
that room to play and go this way but I

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:48.840
know what's coming next because I've
already thought of it you know kind of

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:52.950
thing so the first draft I think is
really important just to play devil's

